+
+# Design analysis for the D17 portal variant
+# ------------------------------------------
+# concretization:
+# - The teal source square and the cropped-away legend remain from the original task.
+# - The four obstacle rectangles are still edge-touching bars, but they now appear as
+#   two same-color pairs rather than 3-4 distinct turn blocks.
+# - One color is used by the top/bottom pair, one color by the left/right pair.
+# - When a sz x sz beam would enter one portal rectangle, it does not turn. Instead it
+#   reappears just beyond the mate rectangle on the far side, keeps its direction, and
+#   then continues painting from there.
+# - Example: a northbound beam that reaches the top portal of color A reappears above
+#   the bottom portal of color A and keeps moving north. Because the paired spans are
+#   laterally shifted, the post-teleport lane misses the original top portal, so the
+#   path does not degenerate into an immediate loop.
+#
+# complexity calibration:
+# - Using exactly two portal colors keeps the pairing rule visible while avoiding the
+#   clutter of 6-8 repeated rectangles.
+# - Each sample always contains four actual teleports, one from each cardinal beam, so
+#   the D17 behavior is always observable rather than being a rare branch.
+# - The legend now repeats the two active colors twice. That duplication is a visible
+#   cue that colors should be paired instead of interpreted independently.
+#
+# hidden assumptions review against the original main():
+# - `_parse_legend(...)` originally assumed four distinct colors because each color
+#   mapped to one turn. That is invalid now: duplicate legend colors are required.
+# - `legend_turn[color]` and `_rot_*` encoded the old obstacle effect. They must be
+#   removed because same-color rectangles would otherwise collapse into one turn rule.
+# - `obstacle_color_at_next(...)` was harmless before because all obstacle hits were
+#   local. With portals, the solver must identify not just the color but also which of
+#   the two same-color rectangles was hit and where its partner is.
+# - The old cycle check still matters, but now generator geometry must guarantee that
+#   post-teleport lanes miss the entry portal and the central square; otherwise a portal
+#   pair could create an infinite shuttle.
+#
+# placement legality:
+# - The generator keeps portals on the four outer borders to preserve the original look.
+# - Source placement is constrained so the top/bottom pair and left/right pair do not
+#   overlap each other at the corners.
+# - Portal spans are chosen by a fixed offset pattern: each pair overlaps the source
+#   lane exactly on the source square, which guarantees the initial hit, and shifts the
+#   exit lane by 2*sz cells, which guarantees a visible jump.
+# - The generator validates all rectangles, simulates the full output, and then checks
+#   `main(input)` against that oracle before accepting a sample.
+#
+# visual observability / neatness / color inference:
+# - The output shows disconnected teal trails that resume on a different lane after a
+#   same-color hit, which is visually unlike the original 90-degree turns.
+# - Only two obstacle colors are active, each repeated in a symmetric pair, so the
+#   matching relation is easy to see across 2-4 examples.
+# - No rule depends on color id ordering or on hidden metadata; only repeated visible
+#   colors and geometry matter.
+
+
+# Color palettes
+LEGEND_COLOR_POOL = [1, 2, 3, 4, 6]
+BG_COLOR_POOL = [1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 7, 9]
+LEGEND_BG = 5
+TEAL = 8
+
+# Canvas dimensions
+GRID_H_RANGE = (19, 24)
+GRID_W = 24
+LEGEND_H = 6
+LEGEND_CELL_STARTS = [1, 7, 13, 19]
+
+# Teal square
+TEAL_SZ2_PROB = 0.75
+
+# Portal geometry
+PORTAL_THICKNESS_RANGE = (2, 3)
+MAX_RETRIES = 400
